  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY NIGHT THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 154 PM CDT Tue Aug 18 2026\n\nThe severe weather threat will likely continue into Thursday, with \nthe greatest potential currently appearing near and east of Highway \n83. A surface trough is forecast to move into the region and should \nprovide a focus for thunderstorm development Thursday afternoon into \nthe evening. Ample instability combined with stronger flow aloft and \nsufficient deep-layer shear should once again support organized \nconvection capable of producing large hail and damaging winds. \nMoisture profiles will also remain favorable, with elevated \nprecipitable water values supporting efficient rainfall processes \nand the potential for locally heavy rainfall with the stronger \nthunderstorms. Some uncertainty remains regarding the exact position \nof the surface trough and where the most favorable instability will \ndevelop, which will ultimately influence the location and magnitude \nof the severe weather threat.\n\nFriday through the weekend, the upper-level ridge across the western \nUnited States will amplify and gradually shift eastward toward the \nregion. Increasing heights and warm air advection will allow 850-mb \ntemperatures to return to the 20 to 25C range across the area. As a \nresult, highs will warm into the upper 80s to lower 90s from late \nweek through the weekend, bringing temperatures back to near to \nslightly above normal for this time of year. Precipitation chances \nshould also decrease as the stronger upper-level flow and better \nforcing shift north and east of the area, while increasing \nsubsidence beneath the ridge limits opportunities for convection. \nOverall, a warmer and predominantly drier pattern is expected \nthrough the weekend.\n\nBy early next week, the upper-level ridge is forecast to begin \nflattening, resulting in a transition toward more zonal flow across \nthe region. While this pattern may eventually allow weak \ndisturbances to move across the area, relatively weak mid and upper-\nlevel flow currently appears likely to limit organized severe \nweather potential. Confidence in the timing and strength of any \ndisturbances remains low at this range.",
